@@ -0,0 +1,106 @@
+---
+title: "Using GetDone with coding agents"
+description: "Use one bootstrapped project state with task or project workflow semantics."
+tags: ["guide", "agents", "codex", "chatgpt"]
+---
+
+# Using GetDone with coding agents
+
+GetDone bootstraps repository state once. Task and project are workflow modes only: they
+control which existing records a coding agent should read and write for the current
+request.
+
+## Bootstrap once
+
+Use the normal full project state when adopting GetDone:
+
+```bash
+getdone init \
+  --project-root . \
+  --profile standard \
+  --skills-root /path/to/skill-pack
+```
+
+Do not re-run bootstrap because a later request is a bounded task or an ongoing project
+goal. The complete `.agent/` structure may exist even when a workflow does not use every
+record.
+
+## Task workflow
+
+Use task workflow for one bounded request such as a bug fix, small feature, refactor,
+documentation change, or reviewable maintenance task.
+
+The agent should read selected GetDone workflow/language guidance, applicable
+`.project-agent/` guidance, stable project facts needed for the request, and prior journal
+entries only when the request or a durable decision makes history relevant.
+
+The agent should write the implementation, applicable validation evidence, one durable
+journal entry, and a durable decision record only when genuinely warranted.
+
+The agent should normally leave roadmap, current task, plans, project status, handoff, and
+`.agent/current/next-step.md` untouched.
+
+Recommended Codex prompt:
+
+> Use GetDone in task workflow for this bounded change. Independently inspect the
+> repository and decide which source files are relevant. Once you understand the likely
+> affected area, run `getdone guidance` with the appropriate task class,
+> `--project-root .`, and known or anticipated affected paths. Follow the returned generic
+> and `.project-agent` guidance, implement and validate the change, then journal the
+> completed work and durable findings. Do not create or update project continuation state.
+> Do not load the full journal unless prior work is specifically relevant.
+
+## Project workflow
+
+Use project workflow for an ongoing development goal spanning multiple tasks or sessions.
+
+The agent uses everything required by task workflow and additionally reads/writes the
+current, planning, evidence, status, reporting, and continuation records required by the
+active goal. `.agent/current/next-step.md` is the authoritative next deterministic step.
+
+Recommended Codex prompt:
+
+> Use GetDone in project workflow for this ongoing development goal. Maintain the existing
+> bootstrapped project state across iterations. Independently inspect the repository, use
+> `getdone guidance` for each bounded implementation task, follow applicable
+> `.project-agent` guidance, run repository validation, journal completed work and durable
+> decisions, and keep current/planning/next-step records accurate for continuation.
+
+## Guidance routing
+
+`--changed-path` is a known or anticipated affected path. The coding agent supplies it
+after enough repository exploration to understand likely scope. GetDone uses it only to
+route workflow and project guidance.
+
+```bash
+getdone guidance \
+  --task-class feature \
+  --project-root . \
+  --changed-path applications/example/lib/settings/page.dart
+```
+
+The returned documents are guidance, not a source-file whitelist.
+
+## ChatGPT
+
+For normal ChatGPT planning, the wheel does not need to be uploaded. Generate the
+appropriate planning prompt locally:
+
+```bash
+getdone planning-prompt --mode project
+getdone planning-prompt --mode execution
+```
+
+For a bounded implementation/review conversation, use task-workflow semantics and provide
+the selected GetDone/project guidance plus the relevant files or diff. For an ongoing goal,
+use project-workflow state and planning records.
+
+## Journal semantics
+
+The journal is historical memory in both workflows. It never owns continuation state.
+
+Task workflow ends after the requested outcome is implemented, validated, and journaled.
+There is no next-step obligation.
+
+Project workflow journals the same history, then separately maintains the project current
+state and next deterministic step.
